<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maximilian Friedrich von Königsegg-Rothenfels</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1708–1784</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maximilian Friedrich von Königsegg-Rothenfels (1708–1784) was Archbishop-Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> from 1761 and Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Münster from 1762 until his death. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ruler guided by his minister Caspar Anton von Belderbusch, he reformed schools and administration from his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>. The young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven">Ludwig van Beethoven</a> played in his court orchestra and dedicated his first published sonatas to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-friedrich-von-konigsegg-rothenfels.jpg" alt="Maximilian Friedrich von Königsegg-Rothenfels" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Heinrich Fischer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurf%C3%BCrst_Max_Friedrich_von_K%C3%B6nigsegg-Rothenfels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
